---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
const project = projects.find(p => p.slug === 'bboss')!;
const chapters = [{id:'context',label:'The product'}, {id:'decisions',label:'Interface decisions'}, {id:'engineering',label:'Shared foundations'}, {id:'outcome',label:'Progress & next steps'}];
---
<Case slug="bboss" chapters={chapters}>
<section id="context"><p class="k">01 / Context</p><h2>A booking is only one part of the experience.</h2><p>A customer wants to find a barbershop and book a service. The business needs to manage attendance, staff and its day-to-day operation. B-Boss brings these different perspectives into one product ecosystem.</p><p>I lead this independent product across UX, interface design and implementation. The scope includes a customer application, a business dashboard, barber and administration interfaces, and a shared API.</p>
<div class="case-brief"><h3>The design challenge</h3><p>Keep each person’s next action clear while connecting the customer journey to business operations.</p></div>
<dl class="audience-map"><div><dt>Customer</dt><dd>Find a barbershop and book a service.</dd></div><div><dt>Business</dt><dd>Understand attendance and daily activity.</dd></div><div><dt>Barber & admin</dt><dd>Work within dedicated operational interfaces.</dd></div></dl>
</section>
<section id="decisions"><p class="k">02 / Design decisions</p><h2>Organize around the person doing the work.</h2>
<figure class="case-figure"><a href={project.image.src} aria-label="Open the B-Boss dashboard capture at full size"><Image src={project.image} alt={project.alt} widths={[600,1000,1600]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 800px" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption>Business dashboard · Fictitious data. <a href={project.image.src}>View full-size capture ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
<p>The business dashboard is a useful cross-section of the product: reporting, navigation and status need to remain understandable together. Three decisions shape that experience.</p><div class="case-decision"><h3><span class="decision-index">01</span> Separate workspaces, shared patterns</h3><p>The customer and business journeys live in separate applications. Shared UI and data packages keep recurring patterns consistent while allowing each workspace to focus on its own tasks.</p></div><div class="case-decision"><h3><span class="decision-index">02</span> Put operational context beside the numbers</h3><p>The dashboard pairs attendance summaries with a date filter, activity over time and unit comparisons. The design makes the reporting period visible so a number can be understood in context.</p></div><div class="case-decision"><h3><span class="decision-index">03</span> Make states distinguishable</h3><p>Confirmed, validated, cancelled and expired passes appear as explicit labels. Color supports the distinction, while text keeps the meaning accessible without relying on color alone.</p></div></section>
<section id="engineering"><p class="k">03 / Engineering</p><h2>Shared foundations across the product.</h2><p>The Nx workspace brings the applications and shared packages together. React and TypeScript power the interfaces; TanStack Router and Query handle routing and server state. The API uses Fastify, Prisma and PostgreSQL.</p><div class="architecture" aria-label="B-Boss architecture"><div>Customer · Business · Barber · Admin</div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div>Shared UI · Schemas · Authentication</div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div>Fastify API → Prisma → PostgreSQL</div></div><p>The trade-off is coordination: changes to a shared contract can affect several applications. Explicit schemas and shared packages make those dependencies visible instead of letting each app define its own version.</p></section>
<section id="outcome"><p class="k">04 / Outcome & boundaries</p><h2>From shared patterns to pilot testing.</h2><p>The product has reached pilot testing. The work brings product flows, implemented interfaces and shared contracts into the same development process. Feedback so far comes from conversations; formal usage and business-impact measurements are still ahead.</p><div class="outcome-grid"><div><h3>What is demonstrated</h3><p>Implemented interfaces, separate workspaces and a shared technical foundation. The source repository is private.</p></div><div><h3>Next validation</h3><p>Validate complete booking and operational journeys with pilot participants, then use observed task completion and feedback to guide the next iteration.</p></div></div></section>
</Case>
